WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.679
An architectural style defines a vocabulary of
components and connectors together with

00:00:04.679 --> 00:00:09.629
constraints on their organization and behavior.
A label such as layered or client–server is

00:00:09.629 --> 00:00:13.981
therefore only the beginning of a description.
Identify what components do, what

00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:17.082
communication means and which arrangements are
permitted.

00:00:17.082 --> 00:00:21.959
The same graph shape can represent different
styles if its edges have different semantics.

00:00:22.082 --> 00:00:27.844
The opening examples combine several ideas:
distributed communication, objects, layers and

00:00:27.844 --> 00:00:33.734
pipelines. Real systems can combine styles, so
classification should clarify a design rather

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:39.368
than force an exclusive label. Ask what problem
a style addresses, which invariants must

00:00:39.368 --> 00:00:42.569
hold and what advantages require those
invariants.

00:00:42.582 --> 00:00:47.510
The slide list includes implicit invocation but
does not develop a dedicated treatment;

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:52.380
event-driven content elsewhere can supply that
connection without implying it is fully

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.515
covered here. Can one system use both repository
and pipeline structures?

00:00:56.582 --> 00:01:01.990
Yes. Different responsibilities or views may
combine styles; explain where each applies and

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:03.832
how their constraints interact.

00:01:03.832 --> 00:01:09.319
A repository architecture centers collaboration
on a shared body of information. Independent

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:14.627
components read or modify that state according
to a common schema. An IDE offers a useful

00:01:14.627 --> 00:01:19.220
example because multiple tools can inspect and
transform program information.

00:01:19.332 --> 00:01:24.631
A blackboard specialization allows cooperating
computations to contribute partial results

00:01:24.631 --> 00:01:29.574
that other computations use, with coordination
organized around the evolving shared

00:01:29.574 --> 00:01:30.288
information.

00:01:30.332 --> 00:01:35.695
Centralized data management can consolidate
access policy, backup and concurrency control.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:41.059
It also creates coupling through the data model:
a schema change can affect many consumers

00:01:41.059 --> 00:01:43.860
even when their code does not call one another.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:49.016
Persistence and distribution are implementation
choices, not automatic consequences of

00:01:49.016 --> 00:01:54.111
drawing one repository box. Ask who owns schema
evolution, how concurrent updates are

00:01:54.111 --> 00:01:59.386
coordinated and whether the repository becomes a
capacity or availability constraint. If

00:01:59.386 --> 00:02:04.602
repository clients never call each other, are
they independent of each other’s changes?

00:02:04.602 --> 00:02:10.069
Not completely. They share schema and data
semantics, so changes can affect other clients

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:11.482
through the repository.

00:02:11.602 --> 00:02:17.031
Filters transform streams and pipes carry output
to subsequent input. In the pure style,

00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:22.707
filters avoid shared state and do not depend on
the identity of adjacent filters. Compatible

00:02:22.707 --> 00:02:27.149
data contracts let stages be replaced or
reordered when semantics allow.

00:02:27.149 --> 00:02:32.609
A pipeline is a linear specialization; batch
sequential processing waits for an entire input

00:02:32.609 --> 00:02:35.992
batch before the next transformation produces
its result.

00:02:36.149 --> 00:02:41.131
The shell example illustrates composition, while
the compiler history shows why pure

00:02:41.131 --> 00:02:46.587
categories can be insufficient. Compiler phases
may follow an ordered pipeline while sharing

00:02:46.587 --> 00:02:52.043
symbol tables or an intermediate representation,
forming a hybrid with repository structure.

00:02:52.149 --> 00:02:57.546
Streaming can support overlap, but throughput,
buffering and synchronization still matter.

00:02:57.546 --> 00:03:02.584
Data conversion between stages introduces cost,
and interactive feedback may require

00:03:02.584 --> 00:03:07.741
additional coordination beyond a one-way
transformational flow. If compiler phases all

00:03:07.741 --> 00:03:12.779
update a shared symbol table, is the design a
pure stateless pipe-and-filter system?

00:03:12.779 --> 00:03:17.233
No. It combines pipeline sequencing with shared
repository state.

00:03:17.279 --> 00:03:22.361
Object-oriented organization groups state with
operations that preserve its invariants.

00:03:22.361 --> 00:03:27.326
Clients should rely on a contract rather than
internal representation. Shared mutable

00:03:27.326 --> 00:03:32.350
objects can still create unexpected interactions
when one client changes state another

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:32.875
observes.

00:03:32.875 --> 00:03:38.245
Encapsulation reduces certain dependencies, but
does not make every implementation change

00:03:38.245 --> 00:03:43.192
invisible or require hard-coded identities in
every caller; indirection can manage

00:03:43.192 --> 00:03:43.855
references.

00:03:43.875 --> 00:03:49.446
Layering organizes services by abstraction and
restricts dependencies between them. Strict

00:03:49.446 --> 00:03:54.646
layering normally permits adjacent interactions;
relaxed designs document deliberate

00:03:54.646 --> 00:04:00.278
bypasses. A layer is logical organization, while
a tier describes deployment separation, so

00:04:00.278 --> 00:04:03.311
several layers may reside in one process or
tier.

00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:08.211
Stable contracts can localize changes, but
effects may propagate when behavior or

00:04:08.211 --> 00:04:13.465
assumptions change. Evaluate dependency
direction and runtime cost instead of assuming a

00:04:13.465 --> 00:04:18.718
fixed upper bound on affected layers. Does a
three-layer codebase require three physical

00:04:18.718 --> 00:04:19.196
servers?

00:04:19.375 --> 00:04:25.145
No. Layers express logical responsibility;
deployment tiers and server count are separate

00:04:25.145 --> 00:04:25.664
choices.
